        '3-5', $q$Wagons on the Trail$q$, $q$Long ago, many families moved west across the country in covered wagons. The trip took months. The trail was bumpy, and the weather could change quickly.

Families did not usually travel alone. They joined a large company of wagons that moved together. Each traveler had a companion to share the work. Being in a group made the long trip safer and less lonely.

Before leaving, some groups would prepay a guide to lead them on the trail. Families also bought and packed flour, beans, tools and blankets. Space in a wagon was small, so they had to choose carefully.

On the trail, travelers worked as a team. When a wagon wheel cracked, people helped rebuild it. At night, they untied the oxen from the wagons so the animals could rest and eat grass.

Sometimes the trail seemed to disappear under tall grass or snow. Guides helped the group find the way again. After many months, the wagons finally reached their new homes.$q$, 'draft')
