-- Passage rg4-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9d1a0fbe-e6dd-5f6b-8364-48d8ae6cf6cb', 'rg4-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Tiny Birds, Big Energy$q$, $q$Hummingbirds are some of the smallest birds in the world. Yet these tiny birds are packed with energy. Their wings beat so fast that they make a soft humming sound. That is how the birds got their name.

A hummingbird's heart is a little drum that never stops. When the bird is flying, its heart can beat more than one thousand times a minute. To keep going, a hummingbird must eat often. It visits hundreds of flowers each day to sip the sweet nectar inside. Its long beak slides into a flower like a straw into a cup.

People sometimes say that a person who eats very little "eats like a bird." A hummingbird, however, eats a lot for its size. It needs all that food to power its busy wings.

Hummingbirds are also skilled fliers. They can hover in one place and even fly backward. Most birds cannot do these tricks.

Some hummingbirds travel very far each year. In the fall, they fly south to warmer places. In the spring, they return north. For such a small bird, that is a giant journey.$q$, 'draft')
