   $q$In paragraph 3, why does the author say the idiom "eats like a bird" does not fit a hummingbird?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The idiom means eating a lot, but hummingbirds hardly eat at all.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-idiom"},{"text":"The idiom is about eating seeds, but hummingbirds only drink nectar.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The idiom is only about people, and a hummingbird is not a person.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-literal"},{"text":"The idiom means eating very little, but hummingbirds eat a lot.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The idiom means eating very little, but hummingbirds eat a lot.$q$, $q$The passage explains that "eats like a bird" describes someone who "eats very little," then says a hummingbird "eats a lot for its size." The meaning of the idiom is the opposite of what the hummingbird does.$q$, 'draft', '9d1a0fbe-e6dd-5f6b-8364-48d8ae6cf6cb')
