-- Passage rg4-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f3ea092c-b68e-571f-87b8-285b2ed6a410', 'rg4-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Riders of the Pony Express$q$, $q$In 1860, sending a letter across the United States could take weeks. A business started the Pony Express to speed things up. Riders on fast horses carried mail between Missouri and California. The trip was about 1,900 miles long.

The riders worked in relays. Each rider galloped to a station, jumped onto a fresh horse, and kept going. The stations were spread along the trail like beads on a string. After many miles, a new rider took over the mail bag. This way, the mail never had to stop for long.

Being a rider was no walk in the park. Riders crossed hot deserts, tall mountains, and wide rivers. They rode in rain, snow, and wind. The riders had to be light, brave, and tough. Many of them were teenagers.

Thanks to the riders, a letter could cross the country in about ten days. That was lightning fast for the time.

Yet the Pony Express lasted only about a year and a half. In 1861, workers finished a telegraph line across the country. Messages could now zip along wires in minutes. The Pony Express was suddenly old news. Even so, people still remember the brave riders who carried the mail through all kinds of weather.$q$, 'draft')
