   $q$In paragraph 3, the author says being a rider "was no walk in the park." What does this idiom mean?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The job was very hard and tiring.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The riders never rode through parks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"The riders walked instead of riding.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-literal"},{"text":"The job was easy and relaxing.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The job was very hard and tiring.$q$, $q$The next sentences list deserts, mountains, rivers, rain, and snow, which show the job was difficult. A walk in the park is something easy, so "no walk in the park" means the opposite.$q$, 'draft', 'f3ea092c-b68e-571f-87b8-285b2ed6a410')
