-- Passage rg4-figurative-language-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('51875581-56e4-51ee-b6f8-322c64214379', 'rg4-figurative-language-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-figurative-language'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Power from the Sun$q$, $q$The Sun gives off a huge amount of energy. Solar panels are tools that catch some of that energy and turn it into electricity.

A solar panel is made of many small parts called cells. When sunlight hits a cell, it knocks tiny particles called electrons loose. These moving electrons make an electric current. That current can power lights, computers, and even cars.

Solar panels work best in bright sun. On a cloudy day, they still make some power, but less. At night, they make none at all. That is why some homes store extra power in large batteries. The batteries save energy for a rainy day, so the lights stay on after dark.

Many people like solar power because the Sun will keep shining for billions of years. Using sunlight does not create smoke, either. A roof covered in panels is like a small power plant that sits quietly in the sun.

Solar panels do have some drawbacks. They cost a lot of money to buy and set up. Still, they can last twenty years or more. Over time, they can save families money on their electric bills. As the saying goes, you have to spend money to make money. For many families, solar panels are a bright idea.$q$, 'draft')
